Tools. A tool is an action the model can call. “Create a table with these rows and columns” fits. The server offers the tool; the assistant decides to call it. Excel in that sentence is the thing being acted on, not the assistant.

Resources. A resource is data the server already has and the client can read, addressed by a URI. The spreadsheet’s existing rows and columns fit. Your users://all resource is the same idea: the user list, not an action that changes it.

Prompts. A prompt is a reusable message template, often with arguments. Your generate-fake-user prompt is one: it fills in a name and hands the client a ready-made instruction. “Predefined capabilities” is a different MCP idea. Capabilities are what the client and server advertise they support, such as tools, resources, prompts, and sampling.

Sampling. The server asks the client’s model to generate text. Your create-random-user tool does that with createMessage: the server does not call a model itself; it asks the client to.

A compact version of the map:

Tool: do something
Resource: read something that already exists
Prompt: start from a saved instruction
Sampling: server asks the client’s model to write something